  "question": {
    "text": "A cup of coffee cools from $90^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ to $80^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ in t minutes when the room temperature is $20^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. The time taken by the similar cup of coffee to cool from $80^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ to $60^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ at the same room temperature is :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "thgME",
      "text": "$\\frac{13}{10} \\mathrm{t}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ve2mI",
      "text": "$\\frac{10}{13} \\mathrm{t}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "epSNX",
      "text": "$\\frac{5}{13} \\mathrm{t}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "4mGeA",
      "text": "$\\frac{13}{5} \\mathrm{t}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "By using average form of Newton's law of cooling\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\frac{90-80}{\\mathrm{t}}=\\mathrm{k}\\left(\\frac{90+80}{2}-20\\right) \\\\\n& \\frac{80-60}{\\mathrm{t}^{\\prime}}=\\mathrm{k}\\left(\\frac{80+60}{2}-20\\right)\n\\end{aligned}$\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\text { (i)/(ii) } \\\\\n& \\frac{10 \\times \\mathrm{t}^{\\prime}}{\\mathrm{t} \\times 20}=\\frac{65}{50} \\\\\n& \\mathrm{t}^{\\prime}=\\frac{65}{50} \\times 2 \\mathrm{t}=\\frac{65}{25} \\mathrm{t}=\\frac{13}{5} \\mathrm{t}\n\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
